BaseClass (Non-Serializable): This class does not implement the Serializable interface. It has a baseValue field and a constructor. 
Crucially, it includes a no-argument (default) constructor. This is essential for the deserialization process.

ChildClass (Serializable): This class extends BaseClass and implements the Serializable interface. It has its own field, childName.
Serialization: 
When a ChildClass object is serialized, only the fields declared in ChildClass (like childName) are written to the output stream directly. 
The fields of the non-serializable BaseClass are not serialized.

Deserialization: During deserialization, the JVM first reconstructs the non-serializable BaseClass by invoking its no-argument constructor. 
If BaseClass did not have a no-argument constructor, a InvalidClassException would be thrown during deserialization. 
After the BaseClass portion is constructed, the deserialization mechanism then populates the fields of ChildClass (like childName) 
from the serialized data. The baseValue from the BaseClass will reflect the value set by its constructor during deserialization, 
not the value it had in the original object before serialization.

